item.description:
arXiv:2611.00836v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^n$, $n\ge3$, be a bounded uniformly convex $C^{3,\gamma}$ domain, where $0<\gamma<1$. We study the limit as the positive Robin parameter $\alpha\to\infty$. For the $2$-Hessian torsion problem, let $U$ be the admissible Dirichlet solution. For all sufficiently large $\alpha$, we obtain a unique admissible Robin solution $u_\alpha$ and establish the optimal-order estimate \[
  \|u_\alpha-U\|_{C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)}
  \le C\alpha^{-1}. \] Moreover, $-\sqrt{-u_\alpha}$ has a uniform positive lower bound on its Hessian, and hence $\sqrt{-u_\alpha}$ is strictly concave. For the normalized eigenvalue problem, let $(\lambda_D,\varphi_D)$ be the normalized negative admissible Dirichlet eigenpair, and assume $\varphi_D\in C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)$. For all sufficiently large $\alpha$, there is a unique normalized negative admissible Robin eigenpair $(\lambda_\alpha,\varphi_\alpha)$, which satisfies \[
  \|\varphi_\alpha-\varphi_D\|_{C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)}
  +|\lambda_\alpha-\lambda_D|\le C\alpha^{-1}. \] The Hessian of $-\log(-\varphi_\alpha)$ also has a uniform positive lower bound. The main analytic tool is a uniform H\"older estimate for linear Robin problems, with an $\alpha^{-1}$ gain for $C^{2,\gamma}$ boundary data. A normalized augmented inverse removes the scaling kernel in the eigenvalue problem. A Schur-complement argument combines the $C^2$ convergence with boundary geometry to prove convexity up to the boundary and anisotropic Hessian lower bounds at the scale ${\rm dist}(x,\partial\Omega)+\alpha^{-1}$. The convergence rate $O(\alpha^{-1})$ for the Robin solution functions is optimal even in $C^0$, as follows from their boundary conditions.

arXiv:2611.01094v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: We study attainment of the best constant in \begin{equation*}
  \int_{\mathbb R^{m+n}}
  \bigl(|\nabla_xu|^2+|x|^2|\nabla_yu|^2+a|u|^2\bigr)
  \geq S_a\left(\int_{\mathbb R^{m+n}}
  (1+|x|^2)^{n/(Q-2)}|u|^q\right)^{2/q}, \end{equation*} where \(m,n\geq1\), \(Q=m+2n\), \(q=2Q/(Q-2)\), and \(a>0\). Using annular interpolation and a transverse-average recurrence with \(L^q\) contraction \(2^{-m/q}\), we decompose bounded sequences into finite, Grushin and Euclidean profiles. Energy and critical mass split, with a power bound in the number of profiles on the remaining mass. We prove compactness of normalized minimizing sequences modulo \(y\)-translations under \(S_a<\min\{S_G,a^{n/Q}S_E(1)\}\), where \(S_G\) and \(S_E(1)\) are the tangent constants. The strict-binding set is empty or an open interval; we prove nonemptiness for \(n=1\) and even \(m\geq6\). At finite positive endpoints of a nonempty binding interval, we characterize attainment by first-order binding gaps. We obtain a finite critical-profile decomposition of Palais--Smale sequences with a remainder vanishing in energy.
